# clusters/@@CLUSTER@@/namespaces/@@NAMESPACE@@/00-namespace.yaml
#
# The tenant namespace for @@NAMESPACE@@ — the home + isolation boundary of ONE Developer Team Agent
# (03 §3, §10; Phase 3). The cluster-admin tier owns tenancy: it proposes this Namespace (and the
# quota + default-deny NetworkPolicy alongside it) via the F4 cascade (propose-developer-team,
# submit-suggestion PR); a human reviews + merges; the customer's CI/CD applies it in the
# `namespaces/` wave — BEFORE `agents/` and, within this directory, before the namespaced identity
# and Agent CR (lexical file order: 00- first). The developer-team agent that lives here is READ-ONLY
# and namespace-scoped; the namespace itself is the blast-radius boundary.
#
# The `kubernetes.io/metadata.name` label is auto-applied by the apiserver (NamespaceDefaultLabelName)
# and is what the sibling NetworkPolicies use to select this namespace; `kube-agents/tenant` marks it
# as a kube-agents-managed tenant for auditing/selection.
#
# POD SECURITY (Phase 5 H-B, 05 §5): every proposed tenant namespace enforces the `restricted` Pod
# Security Standard, so the developer-team agent pod (and any tenant workload) must satisfy runAsNonRoot,
# seccomp RuntimeDefault, drop-ALL capabilities, and no privilege-escalation. `readOnlyRootFilesystem`
# — which restricted-PSS does not cover — is additionally hard-enforced on agent pods by
# policy/vap-agent-pod-hardening.yaml. `warn`/`audit` are set alongside `enforce` so a would-be
# violation is also surfaced (not just blocked).
apiVersion: v1
kind: Namespace
metadata:
  name: @@NAMESPACE@@
  labels:
    kube-agents/tenant: @@NAMESPACE@@
    app.kubernetes.io/managed-by: gitops
    pod-security.kubernetes.io/enforce: restricted
    pod-security.kubernetes.io/enforce-version: latest
    pod-security.kubernetes.io/warn: restricted
    pod-security.kubernetes.io/warn-version: latest
    pod-security.kubernetes.io/audit: restricted
    pod-security.kubernetes.io/audit-version: latest
